1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,400
You understand something well.

2
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:06,360
How a system fits together, or why a design went the way it did.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:09,780
In your head, it's a picture, and the picture moves.

4
00:00:10,506 --> 00:00:12,686
Then you have to explain it.

5
00:00:12,946 --> 00:00:15,706
You draw it on a whiteboard, and it gets erased.

6
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:19,626
You write a document, and the picture flattens into paragraphs.

7
00:00:20,106 --> 00:00:23,486
You book a meeting, and next month you book it again.

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,160
The clearest explanations you've ever watched do something simple.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,760
A diagram builds on screen while a voice explains it.

10
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:36,940
Each piece appears the moment it's mentioned, so you always know where to look.

11
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:44,090
Those videos take days to make, with animation software and a microphone and a lot of

12
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:44,450
patience.

13
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:48,570
So almost nobody makes them for the things they explain at work.

14
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:55,167
With Lyceum you write it instead, as one script in plain text.

15
00:00:55,567 --> 00:00:59,727
Each scene says two things: what's on screen, and what's said.

16
00:01:00,477 --> 00:01:04,057
There's no timeline to drag and no keyframes to set.

17
00:01:04,257 --> 00:01:06,217
Your coding agent does the rest.

18
00:01:08,233 --> 00:01:11,993
Ask it for a video about your caching layer, your onboarding flow,

19
00:01:12,193 --> 00:01:13,213
or your new pricing.

20
00:01:13,513 --> 00:01:18,353
It reads the real sources first: the code, the design documents, the notes.

21
00:01:18,853 --> 00:01:22,113
It drafts the script, and then it stops, so you can review it.

22
00:01:22,863 --> 00:01:26,583
Once you're happy, it gives the script a voice, builds every scene,

23
00:01:26,883 --> 00:01:30,263
checks every scene by looking at stills from it, and renders the video.

24
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:35,627
Every piece of the picture is tied to the words that introduce it.

25
00:01:36,127 --> 00:01:38,847
When I say cache, the cache appears.

26
00:01:39,327 --> 00:01:42,007
And when I say database, there it is.

27
00:01:42,667 --> 00:01:45,427
Edit a sentence, and the animation follows it.

28
00:01:45,927 --> 00:01:47,367
Nothing needs retiming.

29
00:01:48,117 --> 00:01:50,377
Reviewing feels like reviewing a document.

30
00:01:50,857 --> 00:01:54,897
Watch the video, point at a moment, and ask for a change.

31
00:01:55,537 --> 00:01:57,197
Only that scene is rebuilt.

32
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:02,140
An engineer can explain a system to a new teammate.

33
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,980
A designer can walk through why a flow works.

34
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:08,820
A product manager can show how a feature behaves before it ships.

35
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,820
And anyone can tell a postmortem in the order things happened.

36
00:02:13,570 --> 00:02:17,830
Because the agent works from your sources, the video says what's true,

37
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,450
and the script lists where every claim came from.

38
00:02:22,467 --> 00:02:26,887
Everything you just watched started as a script, about four hundred words long.

39
00:02:27,147 --> 00:02:29,127
The voice cost a few cents.

40
00:02:29,877 --> 00:02:31,657
Lyceum is free and open source.

41
00:02:32,037 --> 00:02:35,977
Install it, ask your agent for a video, and share the picture in your head.
